Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  29 REMOVED_MONTH 02 ACCESSIONED ON  29 REMOVED_MONTH 02 at 13 43 CLINICAL DATA  Ultrasound  core biopsy of right breast mass  DDX  Breast cancer                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  4 00   CORE BIOPSY  INVASIVE AND IN SITU DUCTAL CARCINOMA  RECEPTOR STUDIES HAVE BEEN REQUESTED AND WILL BE REPORTED IN AN ADDENDUM  Diagnosis by  Grace T  McKee MD Signed On  31 REMOVED_MONTH 02 at 16 38                                                                                     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right  block A   Few tumor cells stain faintly for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain faintly for progesterone receptor protein  The tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Grace T  McKee MD Signed On  02 AUG 02 at 15 43     IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right  block A   Few tumor cells stain faintly for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain faintly for progesterone receptor protein  The tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  These tests were developed and their performance characteristics determined by the Pathology Department at the Massachusetts General Hospital  They have not been cleared or approved by the U S  Food and Drug Administration  FDA   The FDA has determined that such clearance or approval is not necessary  this laboratory has established and verified the test s accuracy and precision  Added by  Grace T  McKee MD Signed On  06 AUG 02 at 15 11 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number and  right breast   are multiple cores of fibrofatty tissue ranging from REMOVED_DATE x 0 1cm to REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm which are submitted in toto as cassette A  Gross Description by  TRANG HOANG Resident  VINCENT KO  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH OUTPATIENT  OFF P CLINIC  REPORTS TO  Kevin Hughes MD Amy Ellen Wheeler  M D  Dianne Georgian smith  M D  PART   BREAST  4 00   CORE BIOPSY , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Breast CTR Surg WAC 240 MEEI MRN  REMOVED_DATE Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  CONNIE ROCHE  NP KENNY C LAI  M D  RACHEL ANN HITT  M D  Resident  Matija Snuderl  M D  Signed Out by  Ronald Balassanian  M D  CLINICAL DATA  The patient is a 56 year old woman with very dense breast with indeterminate focus of enhancement seen in the medial left breast on MRI  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  A  BREAST  LEFT   CORE BIOPSY 1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  WELL DIFFERENTIATED GRADE REMOVED_DATE 2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  DCIS   MICROPAPILLARY TYPE  GRADE REMOVED_DATE 3  FIBROCYSTIC CHANGE WITH APOCRINE METAPLASIA  SEE NOTE  Note  Sections of the core biopsy reveal a rare cluster of angulated ducts infiltrating the stroma which are present over an area measuring approximately 0 2cm  Adjacent to the lesion is a 0 3cm cluster of ducts which are expanded by a proliferation of monomorphic ductal cells arranged in a micropapillary pattern lining the duct structures  The morphologic findings are compatible with a diagnosis of invasive tubular carcinoma arising in association with low grade micropapillary ductal carcinoma in situ  To help confirm these diagnoses  we performed level sections and a panel of immunostains on the core biopsy  Multiple level sections show the DCIS to better advantage  An immunostain for keratin REMOVED_DATE shows focal loss of staining in luminal cells and supports the diagnosis of DCIS  Immunohistochemical stains for cytokeratin  p63  myosin and calponin performed on slide  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  demonstrate absence of the myoepithelial layer in the infiltrating ducts and supports the diagnosis of invasive ductal carcinoma  Overall  the findings are limited due to limited sampling of the lesion  however  based on morphology and immunohistochemistry  the findings are compatible with well differentiated invasive ductal carcinoma and low grade ductal carcinoma in situ  Dr  David Wilbur has reviewed this case and concurs with the diagnosis  GROSS DESCRIPTION  The specimen is received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN only is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 1cm  aggregate of multiple tan yellow to gray white fibroadipose tissue cores  which are submitted in toto  in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on Monday August 31   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 12 51 13PM By his her signature above  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  PART   Breast  Left    Core biopsy  report_end  , Checked.
