 absence of a myoepithelial layer around small infiltrating ducts  supporting the diagnosis of invasive carcinoma  Deeper level sections of block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  were subsequently performed and demonstrate the invasive and in situ carcinoma to better advantage  The resection margins of the specimen were inked and microscopically evaluated  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast lumpectomy   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 3cm excision specimen containing 2 needle localization wires  and oriented by the surgeon as follows  Short stitch is anterior  long stitch is lateral  bowtie is cephalad  The long axis of the specimen is the medial to lateral axis and the specimen is inked as follows  Anterior blue  posterior black  superior green  inferior yellow  The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 2cm yellow nodule surrounded by fibrous tissue  next to a metal clip  consistent with prior biopsy site  located 0 9cm from the inferior margin  1 7cm from the superior margin  0 9cm from the posterior margin  1 8cm from the anterior margin  0 8cm from the lateral margin  and 2 0cm from the medial margin  The specimen is submitted as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Entirely submitted prior biopsy site  with the area where the metal clip was found in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Fibrous tissue  lateral to biopsy site  from medial to lateral   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Fibrous tissue  medial to biopsy site  from lateral to medial   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial margin  en face   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral margin  en face  Final Diagnosis by Ronald Balassanian M D   Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1 QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FO